VulkanLumen — Stage 6: screen-probe visibility gather

The VulkanLumen public create/record/compose interface is unchanged.
vulkan-experiment remains a fixed test harness, with only GPU screen-pass
timing added to its terminal output.

Inherited from Stage 5
  - Exact rotated Cornell box scene, full-size materials and direct shadows
  - 30-triangle shared-layout static BVH and one direct-shadow ray per fragment
  - RGBA8 16-card material atlas, updated when dirty
  - 12x8x12 world-space directional radiance cache, updated incrementally
  - Default world-cache update budget: 64 probes x 4 rays per frame
  - Full-resolution primary rasterization capped at 1280 pixels wide on Retina

Screen probes (Stage 6)
  - Retains Stage 5 full-resolution lighting as the exact fallback.
  - Rasterized HDR alpha stores the current Cornell surface ID; D32 depth
    is now stored and sampled in a read-only layout after the render pass.
  - One probe per 16x16 internal pixels, e.g. 80x45 at 1280x720.
  - Each probe reconstructs a visible world-space hit position from depth,
    takes the face normal, and gathers incoming light from nearby world probes.
  - At most two extra BVH visibility rays per screen probe reject world-space
    cache samples blocked by other surfaces. The screen-probe result is a
    correction against the Stage 5 world-cache estimate, not new path noise.
  - Two small RGBA16F screen-probe images store lighting correction, surface
    identity and position/linear depth for geometric validation.
  - The final presentation composite samples up to four neighboring probes.
    It accepts contributions only when face IDs and view depths agree; when
    they do not, it displays the unchanged Stage 5 lighting for that pixel.
  - Only diffuse indirect lighting is adjusted. Full-resolution direct
    lighting, shadows, geometry edges and emissive surfaces remain untouched.
  - No per-pixel denoising, neural inference or temporal image accumulation.

Resource and synchronization model
  1. Optional Surface Cache material capture
  2. Incremental world radiance cache compute
  3. Rasterized Cornell HDR color + persistent readable depth
  4. Screen-probe compute dispatch and image write->read barriers
  5. Final presentation composite with validated indirect correction

Performance
  - GPU timing now includes screen=... ms, in addition to cards, GI,
    scene, composite and total.
  - M1's last measured Stage 5 total was around 7.6ms at 60 FPS, with
    FIFO presentation making CPU frame time ~16.67ms.
  - Stage 6 adds up to two visibility rays per 16x16 tile and four small
    light/depth checks per composited pixel. It may increase both the new
    screen pass and the presentation pass. No 60-FPS guarantee until tested.
  - The screen probe stage is rebuilt every frame to avoid stale history
    when the camera moves. The world probes keep their existing bounded
    temporal samples.

Limitations
  - This is a visibility-aware screen-probe gather, not yet Epic Lumen's
    complete screen tracing, adaptive radiance gather or surface-light cache.
  - Probe spacing is fixed. View-dependent screen probes can still lose
    detail at thin surfaces or near sharp silhouettes; the fallback and
    surface/depth validation prevent cross-face blending.
  - The Cornell test uses analytic flat face normals and a fixed grid.
    The external scene/G-buffer handles do not yet implement general meshes.
  - No dedicated reflections, SDF scene tracing, multibounce cache feedback,
    or per-probe depth-moment/relocation system yet.

Test from vulkan-experiment/
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Look for shader build/shaders/lumen_screen.comp and startup:
  [lumen] Stage 6 ... screen probes=80x45 ...
  [lumen] ... GPU cards=... GI=... scene=... screen=... composite=... total=...

Please compare the same camera view with Stage 5 for any light leaking,
screen-tile pattern, excessive darkening or FPS regression. Report screenshot
and several GPU timing lines before Stage 7 (screen ray fallback).
